        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_15-1.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">204</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Benutzt durch Hn 13, 4. 34, 1, näher als Q zum Original; übersetzt durch Leis Wl 39, 1</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Used by Hn 13, 4. 34, 1, closer to the original than Q; translated by Leis Wl 39, 1</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_15-1.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">204</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">‘injust law’ Thorpe; zu allgemein ‘Ungesetzlichkeit (was II 60 passt) begeht’ Schmid. Vielmehr ‘unrichtiges materielles (oder Prozess-)Recht’; laut 15, 3</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">‘injust law’ Thorpe; too general, ‘commits illegality’ (which fits II 60) Schmid. Rather ‘incorrect substantive (or procedural) law’, according to 15, 3</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_15-1.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">204</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Hermann (<hi rend="italic">Schöffengericht</hi> 233) versteht dies ‘oder’ als <hi rend="italic">sive</hi>: ‘indem er ungerechtes Urteil spricht’. Allerdings spricht 15, 3 von <hi rend="italic">rihte lage</hi> allein, so dass jenes beides zusammen Einen Begriff zu bilden scheint; doch vielleicht ist dies nur kürzender Ausdruck. Denn 15, 2 verbindet <hi rend="italic">rihte lage ⁊ rihtne dom</hi> durch <hi rend="italic">and</hi>; und <hi rend="italic">Frencisce men .. unlage rærdon ⁊ undom demdon; s. Gl</hi> Gerechtigkeit 5c. d. 6b. Es können die zwei Wörter recht wohl die beiden Bestandteile des Einen Begriffes ‘Urteil’ angeben wollen, <hi rend="italic">lagu</hi> ‘Rechtsnorm’ und <hi rend="italic">dom</hi> ‘Richterspruch’</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Hermann (<hi rend="italic">Schöffengericht</hi> 233) understands this ‘or’ as <hi rend="italic">sive</hi>: ‘by pronouncing an unjust judgement’. It is true that 15, 3 speaks of <hi rend="italic">rihte lage</hi> alone, so that the two together seem to form a single concept; but perhaps this is only an abbreviated expression. For 15, 2 joins <hi rend="italic">rihte lage ⁊ rihtne dom</hi> with <hi rend="italic">and</hi>; and <hi rend="italic">Frencisce men .. unlage rærdon ⁊ undom demdon; see Gl</hi> Gerechtigkeit 5c. d. 6b. The two words may very well be meant to indicate the two components of the single concept ‘judgement’: <hi rend="italic">lagu</hi> ‘legal rule’ and <hi rend="italic">dom</hi> ‘judicial sentence’</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_15-1.lnote.de.4" n="4" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">204</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">læsio</hi> in Q ungenau durch Gleichklang</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">læsio</hi> in Q is inexact, owing to the similarity of sound</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_15-1.lnote.de.5" n="5" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">204</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">‘Wergeld’ straft auch sonst Bestechung; <hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 3. a; falsches Urteil 1—2.—Inst rechnet wieder um, als wäre Cnuts Schilling nur 4 Pf.</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">‘Wergild’ is the penalty for bribery elsewhere too; <hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 3. a; falsches Urteil 1—2.—Inst again converts the sum, as though Cnut's shilling were only 4 pence</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_15-1.lnote.de.6" n="6" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">204</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">‘Wergeld’ straft auch sonst Bestechung; <hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 3. a; falsches Urteil 1—2.—Inst rechnet wieder um, als wäre Cnuts Schilling nur 4 Pf.</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">‘Wergild’ is the penalty for bribery elsewhere too; <hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 3. a; falsches Urteil 1—2.—Inst again converts the sum, as though Cnut's shilling were only 4 pence</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="IICn_15-1.robnote.n.1" n="1" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">353</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Cf. III Edg. 3; Leis Wl. 39, 1; Hn. 13, 4; 34, 1.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="IICn_15-1.robnote.n.2" n="2" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">353</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Cf. <hi rend="italic">Sax. Chr.</hi> ann. 1052 C, D: <hi rend="italic">Frencisce men…unlage rærdon</hi> <hi rend="italic">⁊ undom demdon</hi>. For the opposite of these terms, see cap. 15, 2: <hi rend="italic">rihte lage ⁊ rihtne dom</hi>.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="IICn_15-1.robnote.n.3" n="3" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">354</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">With the reading of B and G, cf. V As. 1, 3; with that of A (<hi rend="italic">his weores</hi>), cf. II As. 17.</seg></note>
